{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Arial;}}{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue0;\red217\green217\blue217;\red231\green235\blue240;}{\stylesheet{\s0\f0\fs21 Normal;}{\s1\sbasedon0\fs32\b Title;}{\s2\sbasedon0\fs23\b Heading 1;}}\paperw11906\paperh16838\margl1134\margr1134\margt950\margb950\f0\cf1
{\header\pard\fs16 Graph Based Product Recommendation Engine\par}
{\footer\pard\qc\fs17 Scalable Data Science Mini Project  |  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}\par}
\pard\s1\fs32\b\keepn\sa160 Graph Based Product Recommendation Engine\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Scalable Data Science Mini Project Report\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 1 Abstract\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 This project develops a product recommendation engine using implicit customer feedback and compares traditional collaborative filtering with graph algorithms. The Retailrocket event dataset provides product views, cart additions and transactions. After cleaning, chronological splitting and activity filtering, the training set contains 16,053 users, 12,015 products and 152,818 user-product interactions. These interactions form both a sparse matrix and a weighted bipartite graph.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Item-Item Collaborative Filtering, Common Neighbors and Personalized PageRank generate five unseen products for each user. A popularity model provides a non-personalised reference. In the saved experiment on 300 users, PageRank achieves the highest average Precision@5 and NDCG@5, but takes about 504 milliseconds per user. Common Neighbors provides an intermediate quality and latency trade-off. A Streamlit dashboard exposes the recommendations, explanations, graph structure and experimental results.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 2 Introduction and Problem Statement\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Large online catalogues make manual product discovery difficult. A useful recommender should identify products that match a visitor's interests while returning results quickly. Retailrocket contains behaviour rather than explicit ratings, so user interest must be inferred from actions. Sparse histories, unequal product popularity and the appearance of new users make this task challenging.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The project investigates whether indirect relationships in a user-product network improve top-five recommendations compared with item similarity. It also examines the computational cost of these methods. The work is an implementation and comparative study of established algorithms; it does not introduce a new graph learning model.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 3 Aim and Objectives\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The aim is to build an interpretable recommendation pipeline and assess quality, execution time and scalability. The objectives are to clean and weight event data; prevent future information from entering training; construct a bipartite graph; implement three personalised recommenders; evaluate them on identical users; and present the findings in an interactive application.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 4 Scope\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The system recommends anonymised product IDs from the training catalogue. It focuses on offline ranking accuracy and a local demonstration dashboard. Product descriptions, online experiments, purchase conversion prediction, distributed processing and production deployment are outside the implemented scope.\b0\par

\page
\pard\s1\fs32\b\keepn\sa160 Dataset and Data Preparation\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 5 Data Source and Characteristics\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The source is the Retailrocket recommender system dataset, attributed in the repository to Retailrocket on Kaggle. The event log spans 137 days, from 3 May to 18 September 2015. Required fields are timestamp, visitorid, event and itemid. Timestamps are converted to a consistent millisecond representation; visitor and product identifiers are anonymised.\b0\par

