\pard\sa20\par
\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 10 Weighted Bipartite Representation\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The graph contains two disjoint node sets: users U and products P. An edge connects a user to a product only when the user interacted with it during training. User nodes have the prefix U_ and product nodes P_. Edge weights are the capped interaction strengths. The graph is undirected, so recommendation algorithms can move between users and products in both directions.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The saved graph has 28,068 nodes and 152,818 edges. It contains two connected components; the largest includes 27,976 nodes, or approximately 99.67% of all nodes. The average user connects to 9.52 products and the average product to 12.72 users. Bipartite density is about 0.000792, confirming that only a small fraction of all possible user-product links are observed.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 11 Artifacts and Recovery\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The exported files include training interactions, test ground truth, item popularity, the graph pickle, item_similarity.npz and a JSON index mapping IDs to matrix positions. Results include per-user metrics, top-five recommendations and scalability measurements. The engine rebuilds the graph from training interactions if the saved graph cannot be loaded, and refits collaborative filtering when the similarity artifacts are unusable.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 12 Application Features\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Seven dashboard pages cover Home, User Recommendations, Baseline Item-Item CF, Graph Recommendations, Algorithm Comparison, Graph Visualization and Scalability Analysis. Users can select an evaluated visitor or enter an ID. The application shows histories, ranking scores, future-interaction hits, contributing products and similar users. A dark theme styles native widgets, recommendation cards and charts. Live results are cached to reduce repeated work.\b0\par

\page
\pard\s1\fs32\b\keepn\sa160 Recommendation Algorithms\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 13 Item Item Collaborative Filtering\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 A sparse user-item matrix R stores the weighted training interactions. Each product is represented by its column of user weights. The columns are L2-normalised, and pairwise dot products give cosine similarities. Similarities are computed in chunks to avoid constructing the complete dense item-by-item matrix at once. Self-similarities are removed and up to 50 positive neighbours are retained for each item.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 For a target user u and candidate product j, the score is the sum of w(u,i) multiplied by similarity(i,j) across products i in the user's history. Already-seen products are excluded. The five highest positive scores are returned; popularity fills any missing positions. This model supports explanations by identifying which historical products contributed most strongly to a candidate score.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 14 Common Neighbors\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Two users are considered similar when they share products. Their similarity is the number of common product neighbours. The method follows paths from the target user to products and then to other users. It retains the 50 users with the highest shared-product counts. Each unseen candidate receives a score equal to the sum of common-neighbour counts multiplied by those neighbouring users' edge weights to the candidate.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 For example, if two users share three products and the second user has weight 5 on a candidate, that user contributes 15 to the candidate score. This is an illustrative calculation rather than a recorded dataset example. Shared-product counting is unweighted, while candidate contributions use interaction weights. The method is easy to explain, although active users and popular products can influence the ranking strongly.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 15 Personalized PageRank\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Personalized PageRank performs a weighted random walk over the complete bipartite graph. At each step, the walk follows graph transitions with probability 0.85 and restarts at the target user with probability 0.15. Transition probabilities depend on incident edge weights. The resulting stationary probabilities represent how frequently each node is visited under this process.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Unseen product nodes with positive probability are ranked, and the five highest are recommended. The implementation uses NetworkX power iteration with a maximum of 100 iterations and tolerance 0.000001. If iteration fails to converge, the method returns popularity recommendations. Traversing the complete graph for each user makes it substantially more expensive than the local methods.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 16 Popularity and Fallback Behaviour\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The popularity reference orders products by their number of distinct training users, breaking ties by product ID. Personalised methods also use popularity when a user is unknown or has fewer than two historical products. Seen products remain excluded whenever history exists. Zero-score popularity entries can pad short personalised lists, so the evaluation records both fallback and padding behaviour.\b0\par

\page
\pard\s1\fs32\b\keepn\sa160 Evaluation and Experimental Results\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 17 Evaluation Protocol\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The saved experiment selects 300 eligible users with random seed 42 and evaluates every method on the same users and temporal split. The task is to retrieve previously unseen, in-catalogue test items. Precision@5 divides correct recommendations by five. Recall@5 divides correct recommendations by the user's relevant set size. F1@5 is their harmonic mean. NDCG@5 rewards placing relevant products earlier, while Hit@5 indicates whether at least one relevant product appears.\b0\par

