\pard\s0\fs18\qc\sa110 Figure 1  Saved mean Precision at 5 across 300 evaluated users\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 18 Interpretation\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 PageRank has the highest observed mean for precision, recall and NDCG. Its Precision@5 of 2.33% corresponds to 35 relevant recommendations across 1,500 recommendation slots. Its hit rate is 10%, meaning that 30 of the 300 users receive at least one relevant item. Common Neighbors reaches an 8.67% hit rate, Item-Item CF 3%, and popularity 0.67%. The absolute values therefore remain modest despite improvements over the baseline.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 PageRank's mean request time is approximately 1,425 times the CF time and 137 times the Common Neighbors time in this saved run. These ratios describe the reported implementation and hardware, rather than universal algorithm speed ratios. The repository uses 1,000 bootstrap samples for 95% confidence intervals. Comparing individual interval overlap is not a valid substitute for a paired statistical test of per-user differences.\b0\par

\page
\pard\s1\fs32\b\keepn\sa160 Scalability and Validation\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 19 Experimental Design\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Scalability experiments use nested samples containing 10%, 30%, 60% and 100% of the filtered active-user population. Each sample retains those users' training interactions and builds its own CF model and graph. Up to 200 eligible users are evaluated per sample; the smallest sample contains only 82 evaluable users. The dataset percentages refer to active training users, rather than percentages of the entire raw event log.\b0\par

