\pard\s0\fs18\qc\sa110 Figure 2  Saved mean recommendation time as the active user sample grows\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 20 Observations and Measurement Limits\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Edges increase from 16,026 to 152,818, while graph construction rises from approximately 0.107 to 0.620 seconds. PageRank time increases from 106.0 to 511.7 milliseconds per user. CF and Common Neighbors remain much faster, although their averages vary with the sampled graph and users. These four sizes demonstrate behaviour within the tested range; they do not establish performance on much larger production datasets.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Graph memory is estimated from process RSS before and after construction. The 10% sample reports zero additional megabytes because allocator reuse can hide incremental allocation. These measurements are approximate, not exact graph sizes. Different test users across sample sizes also prevent treating quality changes as a controlled effect of dataset size alone.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 21 Validation Record\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The saved Phase 1 test file records 17 passing checks covering preprocessing, temporal separation, graph counts and weights, recommendation validity, fallback behaviour, probability normalisation and metric consistency. During local review, both saved models loaded, 20 sampled live lists matched saved recommendations, and quality metrics were independently reproduced from the saved rankings and ground truth. All seven dashboard pages subsequently passed headless execution checks. The complete pytest suite was not run because pytest was unavailable; the saved checks and local checks are separate evidence.\b0\par

\page
\pard\s1\fs32\b\keepn\sa160 Limitations Conclusion and References\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 22 Limitations\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Evaluation covers a selected group of returning, active users with eligible new products. Only 861 users qualify, compared with more than 308,000 visitors in the test period. New users receive non-personalised popularity recommendations, so the model comparison does not establish strong cold-start performance. Product IDs are anonymised and the implementation does not use product descriptions or categories.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 All test event types count as relevant, even though training weights assign different strengths to views, carts and transactions. Ranking accuracy therefore measures future interaction retrieval rather than purchase prediction. The edge cap, activity thresholds, neighbour counts and damping factor are fixed settings, without a reported separate validation-period tuning study. Strong accuracy claims require broader evaluation and paired statistical analysis.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 NetworkX stores Python graph objects in memory and PageRank recomputes a graph-wide vector for each uncached user. This limits request throughput and dataset scale. The application is a local experimental dashboard, without demonstrated concurrent load handling, online feedback collection or production monitoring. Saved timings must not be interpreted as current laptop benchmarks.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 23 Future Work\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 A stronger study should evaluate all eligible users or multiple random samples, use a separate temporal validation window and report paired bootstrap confidence intervals for metric differences. Additional metrics such as catalogue coverage, diversity, novelty and results by user-history length would clarify recommendation behaviour. Purchase-specific ground truth would test a distinct business objective.\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 Engineering improvements include sparse or approximate PageRank, precomputed candidate sets, incremental artifact refreshes and performance tests under concurrent requests. Adding product content could support new-item recommendations. Larger experiments should use fresh processes for memory measurements and hold evaluation cohorts fixed where possible.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 24 Conclusion\b0\par

\pard\s0\fs21\sa105\sl250\slmult1 The project implements a complete path from implicit event data to an explainable recommendation dashboard. Graph methods achieve higher average ranking quality than Item-Item CF in the saved experiment, at greater computational cost. PageRank is the strongest measured ranker, while Common Neighbors offers a practical compromise for the tested workload. The findings support an educational comparison and motivate further validation before deployment.\b0\par

\pard\s2\fs23\b\keepn\sb150\sa70 25 References and Attribution\b0\par

